### C2 — Make clean-replay and visible-output status transitions explicit

The format/runtime distinction in lines 9, 27, and 47–60 is sound. `nbformat` 5.11 describes notebook cells and saved output fields; it is an interchange format, not an execution history or dependency graph. NBClient v0.11 documents sequential execution, hooks, timeouts, and error behavior. Jupyter Client 8.10.0 / message protocol 5.5 provides session and message IDs and parent-header correlation; the application still needs to map each execute request to its own notebook cell/run identity. None of these mechanisms makes a replay hermetic or proves that an output is reproducible. (C1–C3.)

Tighten the proposed state contract so failure cannot leave an older saved output looking current:

- Associate each result with `(notebook, cell_id, code_digest, run_id, kernel_generation)` plus its source, contract, environment and permission fingerprints. Assign the cell ID in the client when it sends the execute request; use message/session correlation to collect replies, not `execution_count` as lineage.
- Publish a new clean-run generation as current only after the run completes successfully and its inputs still match. During a run, retain the last committed generation separately. On failure/cancel/restart, mark outputs from the attempt failed or incomplete; preserve previous outputs as stale/unverified. Also mark cells not reached in an interrupted run stale when upstream inputs/code changed. Record a cancellation or restart event and terminate the process group as proposed.
- Define “current” operationally as a completed clean run whose *complete captured dependency set* still matches. It is evidence of agreement with that recorded run, not a promise of deterministic results.

The proposal already conservatively stales a changed cell and all later code cells because Python’s dynamic state defeats reliable static dependency inference. Preserve that policy. Specify the transitions above as a testable table, including old-output visibility, rather than relying on the four status labels alone.

**Affected dependency:** client-side run journal, output-generation commit logic, UI status model and comparison view. Validation is proposed/UNEXECUTED.

### C3 — Close the gap between declared inputs and files the kernel can read

Lines 25, 47, 58, 70, and 72 rely on complete data/environment fingerprints and say undeclared file access should fail. A read-only mount protects files from writes; it does not prevent Python from reading another file inside a broadly mounted project tree. If such a file, local module, configuration file, or environment-dependent path affects a result without being recorded, a source hash and lockfile can still match while the output differs.

Choose and qualify a dependency-visibility rule. A defensible first version is to expose only explicitly granted input paths and the read-only runtime, fingerprint every exposed input, use a separate writable result/temp area, deny all other host paths and network by default, and mark a run unverified if it depends on an input the recorder cannot account for. If mounting the whole project is needed for usability, either include digests for its complete readable content in run identity or provide reliable file-read tracking; do not assume a static notebook dependency graph discovers dynamic `open`, imports, symlinks, subprocess reads, or environment access. Keep credentials, home directories, and host sockets inaccessible as proposed.

A source hash taken before a run also needs a mutation-race policy. Hold/open a stable read-only snapshot for the run, or verify content identity after the read and refuse Current if it changed. For large external files, report the cost of full hashing and distinguish provisional metadata identity from verified content identity as the proposal already plans.

**Affected dependency:** what a kernel can see, file/source identity, environment fingerprint, cache keys, stale propagation and the meaning of Current. This is an engineering inference and product decision; the existing proposal does not establish an implementation for tracing all reads.

### C4 — Apply the same OS boundary to SQL, and name the quota mechanism

The Linux-first Bubblewrap choice is appropriately described as a sandbox *constructor*, not a ready-made policy. Its README says the caller’s mounts/namespaces determine the boundary and that mounted files remain part of the attack surface. However, line 70 lists CPU, memory and process-count limits without naming an enforcing component. Bubblewrap’s documented interface does not itself supply aggregate cgroup quotas. Linux cgroup v2 provides CPU, memory and PID controllers, but availability/delegation on the supported desktop must be tested; disk, output-size and wall-time limits also need explicit enforcement (for example, filesystem quota/temp-volume limits, a bounded writer, and a supervisor timer). Do not claim the quota policy is effective until the supported host can enforce it. (C11, C13.)

Optional DuckDB SQL is still executable, untrusted input. DuckDB’s current 1.5 security guidance warns that SQL can read files and treats untrusted SQL like code; disabling external access also disables file operations needed for ordinary imports, and path settings alone are not a complete OS boundary. Therefore run SQL transforms inside the same restricted process/OS boundary as Python, or provide an equally qualified isolated executor. Bind approved data as read-only inputs, constrain or disable extension loading and external paths/network, and test policy escape attempts. Do not execute arbitrary SQL through a privileged in-process engine merely because it is called a data transform. (C12.)

If resource isolation or input scoping is unavailable on a host, disable arbitrary Python and SQL execution there and keep project viewing/import/export available, as the proposal already suggests for unqualified operating systems. Bubblewrap’s `main` README capture is mutable and no binary version is pinned; qualify a specific binary/build and supported kernel/host configuration before relying on its behavior. An import blacklist is correctly rejected by the proposal and does not fill these gaps.

**Affected dependency:** executor architecture, feature gate, host support statement, DuckDB SQL integration and security qualification. Sandbox, quotas and SQL isolation remain proposed/UNEXECUTED.

### C5 — Define row locators in terms of parsed records, not physical lines

Line 40’s content-digest plus ordinal is a useful default for locating a record without falsely treating row values as identity. Clarify “physical record ordinal”: use the **logical record ordinal under the versioned parser/contract**, not a physical text-line number. A quoted CSV record may span lines. For partitioned/multi-file input include the immutable file/object identity as well as the parser version and record locator; for Parquet, row-group plus row ordinal locates bytes in that specific immutable file, not a durable business identity across rewrites. A declared business key remains a separate identity claim and needs the proposed null/uniqueness checks.

Preserve the proposal’s distinction between source order and result order. A preview can say it shows source order for the bounded records it selected; SQL results have no order promise without `ORDER BY`, and stable ordering needs a unique tie-breaker. DuckDB’s 1.5 workload guidance documents that disabling insertion-order preservation can reduce memory in some import/export workloads while allowing reordering, but does not prove the target workload’s performance. (C5.)

**Affected dependency:** diagnostics/quarantine locations, stable row references, preview labels, comparisons and transform lineage. The precise locator rules are an engineering contract to specify and test.

### C6 — Keep persistence claims narrower than the SQLite evidence

Immutable output generations and a small pointer/manifest commit are a reasonable design. SQLite’s atomic-commit page describes rollback-journal behavior and its filesystem/hardware assumptions; SQLite’s WAL page separately documents the WAL/SHM sidecars, checkpointing, same-host restriction and one-writer behavior. Those pages support the optional rebuildable local index and the warning not to copy a live WAL database without its associated state. They do **not** qualify the proposal’s cross-platform generation protocol, directory flush, atomic rename, sync-folder behavior, or crash recovery. (C9–C10.)

Retain the JSON/JSONL-only alternative and the last-committed-generation model, but state the guarantee conditionally and test each supported local filesystem. Specify ordering of artifact writes, file and directory flushes where available, manifest replacement, and recovery when a write or flush fails. A portable bundle should be assembled from a closed/checkpointed index or omit the disposable index; never package a live WAL database as only its main file. OS locks plus compare-before-save are a sound local policy candidate, but sync-folder clients and network filesystems may not honor the same locking/rename assumptions; detect conflicts or generate a fork on observed digest mismatch. Crash injection, lock behavior and bundle recovery remain unexecuted.

**Affected dependency:** save/reopen, concurrency, exchange, local index and “last successful result” claims.

### C7 — Bind the environment claim to the actual replay inputs

The proposal properly says `uv.lock` is a candidate rather than a cross-machine guarantee, and requires a Python build/platform and artifact hashes after qualification. Current uv sync documentation distinguishes the lockfile from the act of syncing an environment; the inspected page does not establish an exact uv release or that native wheels/system libraries are available on another machine. Keep this as a candidate tool, and pin and record the tool/build, interpreter, platform/architecture, kernel/client, DuckDB build/extensions and dependency artifacts used by a supported project. Track relevant execution settings (working directory, locale/timezone, allowlisted environment values, seeds and engine options); if a value cannot be reproduced or captured, downgrade the result to unverified. (C2, C3, C12, C14.)

**Affected dependency:** project exchange, clean replay, cache identity and dependency/provenance UI. Exact shipped pins remain unresolved by the evidence and were correctly left for qualification.

## Supported claims to preserve

- The workflow is covered at the level appropriate for this research: create a folder/project manifest; import by reference with explicit copy/vendor choice; inspect a bounded preview; author Python and optional SQL transforms; run selected cells interactively; request a new-kernel sequential replay; show progress, errors and cancellation; compare provenance/results; save a committed generation; reopen/exchange ordinary folders or a portable bundle; export results plus a small manifest. The proposal is a plan, not proof these flows exist.
- `.ipynb` + Jupyter are a useful document and execution-protocol precedent; DuckDB is an independently useful local tabular engine precedent. Neither supplies integrated provenance, sandboxing, or application guarantees. Optional SQL in DuckDB’s dialect is a defensible narrow path for the colleague; it avoids building every language kernel while preserving the need to isolate SQL.
- The import contract’s explicit parser/version, encoding, null/missing rules, conversions, timestamp policy, schema evolution, Unicode handling and fail-on-error default are well scoped. Keep raw source bytes unchanged. A preview/sniffer proposes a schema and bounded sample only; it cannot establish a full-input invariant. Malformed rows must be reported or explicitly quarantined with identity/reason, never silently deleted.
- DuckDB scanning and bounded batches are sensible candidates for a 5 GB input, with global operations such as joins/sorts allowed a separately declared limit/spill requirement. The 8-core/16-GB target is correctly labeled unverified; the cited tuning page is not a benchmark. Cache only fully declared deterministic work, with complete input/contract/environment keys and descendant invalidation.
- The core invalidation rules are sensible: edit to upstream code conservatively stales that cell and later cells; data, contract or environment changes stale affected consumers/descendants; restart/interruption cannot preserve a current label. Preserve old artifacts for comparison and identify truncation. The status needs the explicit transition details in C2.
- The small editable CSV sniff proposal, JSON manifest/event log without SQLite, Linux-first support boundary, and option to delay arbitrary execution pending sandbox qualification are useful opportunities/alternatives. Real-time collaboration, hosted execution, scheduling and broad kernels remain optional.

## Check accounting and validation dependencies

The predecessor’s W1 is recorded as **executed by candidate** in its witness catalog. Its code/input/receipt report a tiny isolated standard-library check over three synthetic NDJSON lines: two accepted, one invalid timestamp retained with raw text and ordinal, Unicode and missing-vs-null assertions, and a cache-key change assertion. This reviewer did not re-execute it. The “unchanged source” assertion hashes an in-memory byte string, not a file; W1 does not test DuckDB, a notebook, filesystem immutability, production parser behavior, security, or scale. Treat it as a narrow component check only.

All predecessor PV1–PV5 checks are **proposed/UNEXECUTED**. Preserve them and gate implementation claims on results, adding the following discriminating cases:

1. Parser/contract corpus for CSV multiline quoted records, escape configurations including the PR regression, NDJSON missing/null and malformed records, Parquet partition evolution, timestamps, Unicode and unsupported compression/encoding. Verify reported logical locators and prove the original file hash remains unchanged.
2. A dependency-visibility test where a notebook attempts to read an undeclared file inside the project, a symlink target, a local module, environment value and network endpoint. Verify deny/record/downgrade behavior and source mutation detection during replay.
3. Replay-state matrix for out-of-order interactive cells, upstream edits, failed/cancelled runs, untouched downstream cells, restart, missing package, changed input and repeated clean run. Confirm the last committed generation is retained while no old result appears current.
4. Separate Python and DuckDB SQL security/resource tests on each supported host, including filesystem/network/extension access, subprocesses, aggregate memory/PID/CPU, wall time, output and temporary disk. Verify feature gating if cgroup or equivalent quotas cannot be enforced.
5. 5 GB representative scan/filter/projection/aggregate/join/sort measurements for elapsed time, peak RSS and temp use on the stated 8-core/16-GB target; label unsupported global work with its tested bounds. This is a future acceptance test, not an existing result.
6. Crash injection at generation writes/flushes/manifest replacement, simultaneous local writers, stale sync-folder copies, close/checkpoint and bundle transfer/reopen. Accessibility testing for keyboard and screen-reader status/error/cancel flow and visibly partial/truncated outputs.

